{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"unpleasant-customable-gyrfalcon","creation_channel":"cli","description":"MEASURED on a real run, not inferred.\n\n## What was observed\n\nA full `rebar review-plan 5e75-522e-70d1-4363` at origin/main 144bb02251, with REBAR_USAGE_LOG\nexported, emitted exactly two `warn_if_cache_ineffective` warnings. Classifying all 37 usage rows\nagainst the 4096-token floor:\n\n  * 7 rows  caching WORKED (nonzero cache_read or cache_write). The Pass-1 finder calls wrote\n            5781-9385 cache tokens each and read up to 98619.\n  * 28 rows BELOW the floor with zero/zero — correctly silent since bug 7a79 landed.\n  * 2 rows  ABOVE the floor with cache_read=0 AND cache_write=0:\n              step `coach_notes`, anthropic:claude-sonnet-4-6, input_tokens 4538\n              a sub-call,        anthropic:claude-haiku-4-5,   input_tokens 6426\n\nThe warning is telemetry-only and behaves correctly here — these are TRUE POSITIVES, which is what\nmakes them worth a ticket rather than a tuning knob. On the same run, in the same process, against\nthe same provider, other calls cached fine. So this is not \"caching is unavailable\"; it is specific\ncall sites getting none.\n\n## Why this is a cost defect, not noise\n\n`cache_write=0` means nothing was even WRITTEN, so there is no first-call-populates-the-cache\nexplanation: every future call with the same prefix also pays full price. Anthropic prompt caching\nhas a model-dependent minimum cacheable prefix, and both observed calls (4538 and 6426 tokens) sit\nabove the documented minimums for their models, so prompt size does not explain the miss either.\n\n## What is NOT yet established\n\nThe root cause. The obvious hypotheses are (a) these paths set `cache_settings` but never place a\ncache_control breakpoint, so nothing is marked cacheable, or (b) the prefix they send is not stable\nbetween calls so no reusable block exists. Both are plausible from the evidence and NEITHER has been\ntested. Do not start from the code: capture the actual outbound request for one of these two calls\nand look at whether a cache_control marker is present, one layer below where the decision is thought\nto be made.\n\nNote the single global floor `capabilities.CACHE_MIN_PREFIX_TOKENS = 4096` is documented as the OPUS\nfloor but is applied to every model. That is a separate question from this one and is not asserted\nhere as a defect — it is flagged only so an investigator does not conflate the two.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The root cause is proven by a captured outbound request for one of the two named call sites\n      showing whether a cache_control breakpoint is present, not by code reading alone.\n- [ ] A test pins the intended caching behaviour for the affected call sites and fails before the fix.\n- [ ] [operator-attested] a real `rebar review-plan` run after the fix shows nonzero cache_write for\n      the previously-zero call sites, cited from REBAR_USAGE_LOG with the run sha and timestamp.\n- [ ] make lint and make typecheck pass by exit code; the default selection is green.","id":"f81d-bab0-3938-4f1c","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Two plan-review sub-calls request prompt caching and receive none, paying full input price on every run"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785703046720745001,"uuid":"ac624148-0f39-4018-a6bf-f8f441adabde"}